# name: test_text_detail_matches_snapshot[MD001]
  '''
  MD001 heading-depth
  Default severity: error
  Pack: builtin
  Enabled by default: yes
  Preview: no
  Fixable: no
  Fix mode: manual
  
  Summary:
    Report heading depth jumps greater than one.
  
  Explanation:
    The first heading may use any depth. A later heading may descend by any amount but may not increase by more than one level at a time, because a skipped level makes the document outline ambiguous for readers and assistive technology. The diagnostic covers the whole heading node, marker included.
  
  Targets:
    syntaxes: markdown
    kinds: heading
    scope requires: None
    owner kinds: unrestricted
  
  Capabilities:
    STRUCTURE
  
  Policy/enablement:
    Selected by default. `MD` selects this rule and `MD001` selects only it. `lint.ignore = ["MD001"]`, a matching `lint.per-file-ignores` entry, or removing `builtin` from `plugins` removes the rule from the enabled set before it runs. A `stilyagi: disable MD001` directive instead suppresses reported findings after evaluation, so a suppressed heading remains the depth the next heading compares against.
    TOML:
    [tool.stilyagi.lint]
    select = ["MD"]
  
  Fixes:
    The rule reports a manual suggestion. The correct heading level is a prose decision, so no edit is applied automatically and neither `--fix` nor `--unsafe-fixes` changes the heading.
  
  Options:
    None
  
  Examples:
    A skipped level is reported
      source:
        # Start
        ### Detail
      configuration:
        defaults
      expected codes: MD001
      expected exit status: 1
  
    Consecutive levels are accepted
      source:
        # Start
        ## Detail
        ### Example
      configuration:
        defaults
      expected codes: None
      expected exit status: 0
  
  Limitations:
    Only headings with a locatable node span are reported; a heading the extractor cannot map to source bytes is skipped.
    The rule compares depth only: it does not enforce a document outline or a maximum depth, and a document may begin at any level.
    Configuration, per-file ignores, and suppression directives can stop a finding; static metadata does not describe effective enablement for a particular file.
  
  References:
    docs/rfcs/rfc-0002-stilyagi-python-rule-api.md
    docs/stilyagi-design.md#72-python-rule-api
    docs/adrs/adr-0012-adopt-the-source-backed-builtin-rule-contract.md
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_text_detail_matches_snapshot[STY001]
  '''
  STY001 repeated-word
  Default severity: warning
  Pack: builtin
  Enabled by default: yes
  Preview: no
  Fixable: yes
  Fix mode: unsafe
  
  Summary:
    Report an immediately repeated word in prose.
  
  Explanation:
    Two or more identical alphabetic tokens separated only by spaces or tabs are reported as a repeated run. Comparison is exact, so `the the` is a run and `The the` is not. A run never crosses markup, a region boundary, a line break, or a synthetic region, and inline code is excluded. The `TOKENS` capability is supplied by the builtin pack's in-process tokenizer; no NLP model or external provider is loaded.
  
  Targets:
    syntaxes: markdown
    kinds: heading, paragraph, table_cell
    scope requires: None
    owner kinds: unrestricted
  
  Capabilities:
    STRUCTURE
    TOKENS
  
  Policy/enablement:
    Selected by default. `STY` selects this rule and `STY001` selects only it. `lint.ignore = ["STY001"]`, a matching `lint.per-file-ignores` entry, or removing `builtin` from `plugins` removes the rule from the enabled set before it runs. A `stilyagi: disable STY001` directive instead suppresses reported findings after evaluation; the rule still evaluates over the document.
    TOML:
    [tool.stilyagi.lint]
    select = ["STY"]
  
  Fixes:
    The rule may propose an unsafe edit that removes the repeated suffix. Apply it only with `stilyagi check --fix --unsafe-fixes`; `--fix` alone skips it, and the `lint.fixable` policy still decides whether the code may contribute an edit. Review the diff before applying it, because a deliberate repetition such as `had had` is removed just as an accidental one is.
  
  Options:
    None
  
  Examples:
    An adjacent repeat is reported
      source:
        the the plan is clear.
      configuration:
        defaults
      expected codes: STY001
      expected exit status: 0
  
    Exact spelling is required
      source:
        The the plan is clear.
      configuration:
        defaults
      expected codes: None
      expected exit status: 0
  
    A line break ends the run
      source:
        the
        the
      configuration:
        defaults
      expected codes: None
      expected exit status: 0
  
  Limitations:
    Matching is exact and adjacent: `the the` is a run, but `the plan the plan` is not because other words intervene.
    A deliberate repetition is reported the same way as an accidental one, and the unsafe edit cannot tell them apart.
    Only headings, paragraphs, and table cells are inspected; inline code, code blocks, and unsupported regions are not.
    Synthetic segment text that cannot be mapped to source bytes is not editable, so no edit is offered for it.
    Configuration, per-file ignores, and suppression directives can stop a finding; static metadata does not describe effective enablement for a particular file.
  
  References:
    docs/rfcs/rfc-0002-stilyagi-python-rule-api.md
    docs/stilyagi-design.md#72-python-rule-api
    docs/adrs/adr-0012-adopt-the-source-backed-builtin-rule-contract.md
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_text_detail_matches_snapshot[TERM001]
  '''
  TERM001 banned-term
  Default severity: error
  Pack: builtin
  Enabled by default: no
  Preview: no
  Fixable: no
  Fix mode: none
  
  Summary:
    Report configured banned terms in prose.
  
  Explanation:
    Every configured term is matched against whole alphabetic tokens, so `obviously` never matches inside `unobviously`. The `TOKENS` capability is supplied by the builtin pack's in-process tokenizer; no NLP model or external provider is loaded. When `case_sensitive` is false, which is the default, both the configured term and the token are compared with Unicode `casefold()`; when it is true, the exact spelling is compared. Duplicate terms coalesce under the chosen comparison. The rule is opt-in and does nothing until terms are configured.
  
  Targets:
    syntaxes: markdown
    kinds: heading, paragraph, table_cell
    scope requires: None
    owner kinds: unrestricted
  
  Capabilities:
    STRUCTURE
    TOKENS
  
  Policy/enablement:
    Disabled by default. Select `TERM001`, or the `TERM` prefix, and configure `terms` under `[tool.stilyagi.rule.TERM001]`. The builtin plugin gate still applies: `plugins = []` disables all builtin rules, and `lint.ignore`, per-file ignores, and suppression directives still apply. Without a nonempty `terms` list the rule is inactive even when selected.
    TOML:
    [tool.stilyagi.lint]
    select = ["MD", "STY", "TERM001"]
  
    [tool.stilyagi.rule.TERM001]
    terms = ["obviously"]
    case_sensitive = false
  
  Fixes:
    The rule offers no fix. Choosing a replacement for a banned term is editorial, so no edit is planned and `--fix` leaves the source unchanged.
  
  Options:
    terms
      type: array[string]
      default: []
      description: Words to report when they appear as whole prose tokens.
      constraints:
        - Must be an array of strings.
        - Each entry must be exactly one alphabetic word under `str.isalpha`; spaces, punctuation, numerals, and empty strings are rejected.
        - Duplicates coalesce after the configured comparison.
      variants:
        None
  
    case_sensitive
      type: boolean
      default: false
      description: Compare configured terms against tokens using exact case instead of Unicode case folding.
      constraints:
        - Must be a boolean.
      variants:
        false: Use Unicode `casefold()` for the configured term and the candidate token. This is the default.
        true: Compare the configured spelling exactly. Neither mode promises Unicode normalization or locale-aware matching.
  
  Examples:
    The rule is inactive by default
      source:
        This is obviously fine.
      configuration:
        defaults
      expected codes: None
      expected exit status: 0
  
    A selected and configured term is reported
      source:
        This is obviously fine.
      configuration:
        [tool.stilyagi.lint]
        select = ["MD", "STY", "TERM001"]
  
        [tool.stilyagi.rule.TERM001]
        terms = ["obviously"]
        case_sensitive = false
      expected codes: TERM001
      expected exit status: 1
  
    Case-insensitive matching folds case
      source:
        Obviously, this is fine.
      configuration:
        [tool.stilyagi.lint]
        select = ["TERM001"]
  
        [tool.stilyagi.rule.TERM001]
        terms = ["obviously"]
      expected codes: TERM001
      expected exit status: 1
  
    Case-sensitive matching requires the exact spelling
      source:
        obviously fine.
      configuration:
        [tool.stilyagi.lint]
        select = ["TERM001"]
  
        [tool.stilyagi.rule.TERM001]
        terms = ["Obviously"]
        case_sensitive = true
      expected codes: None
      expected exit status: 0
  
    Hyphenated text is split into tokens
      source:
        This is obviously-not fine.
      configuration:
        [tool.stilyagi.lint]
        select = ["TERM001"]
  
        [tool.stilyagi.rule.TERM001]
        terms = ["obviously"]
      expected codes: TERM001
      expected exit status: 1
  
    Unicode case folding applies to whole tokens
      source:
        STRASSE matters.
      configuration:
        [tool.stilyagi.lint]
        select = ["TERM001"]
  
        [tool.stilyagi.rule.TERM001]
        terms = ["straße"]
        case_sensitive = false
      expected codes: TERM001
      expected exit status: 1
  
  Limitations:
    Only headings, paragraphs, and table cells are inspected; code blocks, inline code, link destinations, and frontmatter are not.
    Matching is whole-token only, but hyphens, punctuation, and digits are token boundaries: a term can match one word inside a hyphenated or punctuated form. Use an exact context check when that is too broad.
    The opposite editorial policy, requiring a term rather than banning it, is not supported; no inverse rule or option exists in this slice.
    Neither case mode promises locale-aware matching or Unicode normalization; use `case_sensitive = true` when exact spelling matters.
    Configuration, per-file ignores, and suppression directives can stop a finding; static metadata does not describe effective enablement for a particular file.
  
  References:
    docs/rfcs/rfc-0002-stilyagi-python-rule-api.md
    docs/stilyagi-design.md#72-python-rule-api
    docs/adrs/adr-0012-adopt-the-source-backed-builtin-rule-contract.md
  
  '''
# ---
# name: test_text_list_matches_snapshot
  '''
  CODE     NAME                 SEVERITY  FIX-MODE  DEFAULT  PREVIEW
  DOC001   summary-separation   warning   none      no       no
  DOC002   summary-punctuation  warning   none      no       no
  DOC003   owner-only-summary   warning   none      no       no
  MD001    heading-depth        error     manual    yes      no
  STY001   repeated-word        warning   unsafe    yes      no
  TERM001  banned-term          error     none      no       no
  
  '''
